{
 "_comment": "Starter design system for the Greenshift stylebook. Colours are ROLE tokens (primary, secondary, accent, surface, text), so a rebrand is a value change, not a rename: swap the hex values and fonts, keep the names. The -text variants (accent-text, secondary-text) exist because the fill colour fails contrast as text; keep them. a `kind` (color, font-size, spacing, font-family, custom); `stylebook.py push --theme` uses it to register the token as a theme.json preset and alias the gt- name onto it, and an optional `alias` names an existing theme variable to point at instead. A colour token may carry `theme_slug` (one or more Greenlight palette slots such as brand, background, textcolor, heading, lightgrey, card-base, card-text); `push --theme` then writes its value into those slots on the user global-styles record, so the Stylebook Global Color Presets, the editor pickers and the theme CSS all show the brand instead of the theme default. A base layer ships in `elements`: box-sizing, smooth scroll (reduced-motion aware), body full-height + overflow-x clip, link colour and an external-link arrow indicator, paragraph rhythm. Heading look-alike classes (.h1-.h6) and a screen-reader-only .hidden-accessible are in global_classes. Values live as tokens; these are the rules that cannot be drawn in a design and belong site-wide.",
